.TH LAZYDOT 1 "January 2026" "lazydot v0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lazydot \- A minimal, explicit dotfile manager using symlinks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lazydot
[\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] \fIcommand\fR [\fIargs\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lazydot
is a CLI tool that manages dotfiles by creating symlinks from your home directory to a central dotfiles repository. Unlike other dotfile managers that mirror entire directories or auto-discover files, lazydot gives you explicit control over exactly which paths are managed.
.PP
Your actual files live in a \fIdotfolder\fR (e.g., \fI~/gitdot\fR), and lazydot creates symlinks in your home directory pointing to them. This allows you to version control your configurations with git while keeping them in their expected locations.
.SH PHILOSOPHY
.TP
.B Explicit over implicit
You decide exactly which files and directories to track. No auto-discovery, no surprises.
.TP
.B Simple symlinks
Uses standard filesystem symlinks. No copying, no templating, no magic.
.TP
.B Minimal configuration
One TOML file (\fI~/.config/lazydot.toml\fR) lists your managed paths. That's it.
.TP
.B Portable
Your dotfolder is a regular directory. Clone it anywhere, run \fBlazydot sync\fR, done.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B add \fIpath\fR [\fIpath\fR...]
Register one or more paths to be managed. Paths can be files or directories.
.TP
.B remove \fIpath\fR [\fIpath\fR...]
Remove paths from the configuration. Does not delete actual files.
.TP
.B sync
Create or update all symlinks. Copies files to the dotfolder if needed, then replaces originals with symlinks.
.TP
.B status
Show what would be added or removed on the next sync.
.TP
.B check
Display the current state of each managed path (linked, broken, missing, etc.).
.TP
.B disable\-link [\fB\-\-all\fR] [\fIpath\fR...]
Remove symlinks and restore original files from the dotfolder. Use \fB\-\-all\fR to unlink everything.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print help information.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Print version information.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is stored in \fI~/.config/lazydot.toml\fR:
.PP
.nf
.RS
dotfolder_path = "~/gitdot"
paths = [
    "~/.config/nvim",
    "~/.bashrc",
    "~/.config/alacritty"
]

[defaults]
on_duplicate = "overwritehome"
on_delink = "keep"
.RE
.fi
.TP
.B dotfolder_path
Directory where your dotfiles are stored (your git repository).
.TP
.B paths
List of paths to manage. Use \fB~\fR for home directory.
.TP
.B on_duplicate
What to do when a file exists in both home and dotfolder: \fIask\fR, \fIoverwritehome\fR, \fIoverwritedotfile\fR, \fIbackuphome\fR, or \fIskip\fR.
.TP
.B on_delink
What to do with dotfolder copy when unlinking: \fIkeep\fR or \fIremove\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B Add your neovim config:
.nf
lazydot add ~/.config/nvim
lazydot sync
.fi
.TP
.B Add multiple paths at once:
.nf
lazydot add ~/.bashrc ~/.zshrc ~/.config/alacritty
lazydot sync
.fi
.TP
.B Check status of all managed paths:
.nf
lazydot check
.fi
.TP
.B Stop managing a path (keep files):
.nf
lazydot disable-link ~/.bashrc
lazydot remove ~/.bashrc
.fi
.TP
.B Set up on a new machine:
.nf
git clone https://github.com/user/dotfiles ~/gitdot
lazydot sync
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/lazydot.toml
Main configuration file.
.TP
.I ~/gitdot/
Default dotfolder location (configurable).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B non-zero
An error occurred. Error messages are printed to stderr.
